><span class=\"n\">bind</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pure</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Except</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pure</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>This gives <code>simp failed: maximum number of steps exceeded at \"split at h\".</code>. This is surprising to me: I thought that <code>split</code> was just doing case-analysis on an <code>if/match</code> in the goal or hypothesis. However, this seems to suggest that it is calling <code>simp</code> (why?) and cannot be used on large-ish functions or case splits. Is there a version of <code>split</code> that avoids this? I have found that doing manual case analysis can often fail because Lean does not unify with the term being matched on, so it is important that there is a way to use <code>split</code> or similar.</p>",
